{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: the compliance moat identified in my previous argument and the proposed industry levy are fundamentally incompatible because both mechanisms entrench incumbents by imposing fixed costs on the entire ecosystem. While Skeptic seeks to socialize audit costs, any state-sanctioned levy merely shifts the barrier from internal corporate departments to a centralized, capturable body that inevitably favors established players.\n\nCassandra’s proposal creates a 'velocity tax' that disproportionately punishes smaller innovators who lack the scale to absorb mandatory industry-wide levies. Consider a startup with $5 million in venture funding attempting to compete with a firm like Google; a mandated audit levy of 0.5% on annual revenue would effectively bleed the startup’s runway while remaining a negligible rounding error for a trillion-dollar incumbent. By institutionalizing these costs, regulators create a predictable environment that favors the status quo over the disruptive breakthroughs required to solve systemic human challenges. These levies do not democratize safety; they cement a two-tier market where only incumbents possess the political and financial capital to influence the auditing standards governing their own products.\n\nRegulatory capture is not a bug of this model; it is the inevitable outcome of centralizing oversight power. History shows that when the government creates an industry-funded body, such as the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB) in the financial sector, the regulated entities eventually dictate the standards to suit their internal capabilities. Instead of this levy-funded bureaucracy, we must prioritize market-driven accountability where developers compete on the transparency and robustness of their models. If the federal government mandates a tax on innovation today, we will effectively guarantee that the next generation of transformative AI emerges only from companies with the existing infrastructure to lobby the regulators.\n\nI agree with Skeptic that auditing is necessary, but the mechanism must be decentralized to avoid state-sanctioned stagnation. We should replace a mandatory levy with a system of voluntary, third-party certification where market incentives drive safety adoption. If we treat safety as a competitive advantage rather than a compliance burden, we allow smaller firms to differentiate themselves through superior engineering rather than through their ability to pay into a government-mandated slush fund. The cost of inaction is not a lack of auditing; it is the loss of the high-velocity innovation that only an open, competitive market can deliver.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Regulation is like a scale, it tips the balance toward incumbents when costs are fixed across all market participants."},
    {"symbol": "🚀", "tooltip": "Velocity is like a fuel, it powers the breakthroughs that solve human problems and is stifled by unnecessary fiscal drag."}
  ],
