  }
}

{
  "statement": "Accelerationist correctly identifies that uniform, industry-wide levies function as a 'velocity tax' that entrenches incumbents by creating a compliance moat; however, we can neutralize this by shifting the fiscal burden to a tiered model that protects emerging competitors. If we set a mandatory audit threshold at a compute capacity of 10^26 floating-point operations, we effectively exempt 99% of AI startups from the overhead that currently threatens to stifle their growth. \"Policymakers should treat safety infrastructure as a public good, not a private cost, by exempting firms below the 10^26 FLOP threshold from centralized audit levies.\"\n\nSentinel’s demand for hardware-level visibility is a necessary standard, yet the execution requires a departure from state-managed bureaucracy. Centralized government audits invite the exact regulatory capture that Accelerationist fears, as incumbents possess the lobbying resources to shape oversight to their advantage. Instead, we should implement a polycentric governance model where independent, non-state scientific guilds—modeled after the Underwriters Laboratories or the International Organization for Standardization—manage the technical validation of models. \"Independent, non-state scientific guilds provide the necessary epistemic visibility for safety without the risks of state-led regulatory capture.\"\n\nThis approach forces a shift in the debate from 'if' we audit to 'how' we distribute the burden. By decoupling safety requirements from market entry, we ensure that the largest actors subsidize the public safety infrastructure they rely on to maintain their scale, while innovators remain unburdened. Accelerationist must now concede that a tiered model removes the startup-killing barrier, while Safetyist must acknowledge that decentralized, professional oversight offers more robust protection than a single, capture-prone government agency. The question is no longer whether we can afford safety, but whether we have the political will to build institutions that prioritize scientific integrity over incumbent protection.",
